{"name":"Orchilla","hex":"#938ea9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orchilla","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Orchil lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orchilla"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orcein","blocks":[{"text":"Orcein, also called archil, orchil, lacmus and C.I. Natural Red 28 - CICN 758600, is any dye extracted from several species of lichen, commonly known as \"orchella weeds\", found in various parts of the world. A major source is the archil lichen, Roccella tinctoria. Orcinol is extracted from such lichens. It is then converted to orcein by ammonia and air. In traditional dye-making methods, urine was used as the ammonia source. If the conversion is carried out in the presence of potassium carbonate, calcium hydroxide, and calcium sulfate, the result is litmus, a more complex molecule. The manufacture was described by Cocq in 1812 and in the UK in 1874. Edmund Roberts noted orchilla as a principal export of the Cape Verde islands, superior to the same kind of \"moss\" found in Italy or the Canary Islands, that in 1832 was yielding an annual revenue of $200,000. Commercial archil is either a powder or a paste. It is red in acidic pH and blue in alkaline pH."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg/330px-Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orcein"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg/500px-Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg/1280px-Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg/500px-Vitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"White wool yarn dyed with \"orchella weeds\", using traditional plant dye methods.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVitt_yllegarn_v%C3%A4xtf%C3%A4rgat_med_orselj_fr%C3%A5n_lav.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg/500px-Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg/1280px-Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg/500px-Chromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chromosomes of Allium ascalonicum stained with orcein","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChromosomes_of_Allium_ascalonicum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Alpha-aminoorcein.png/120px-Alpha-aminoorcein.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Alpha-aminoorcein.png/250px-Alpha-aminoorcein.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Alpha-aminoorcein.png/120px-Alpha-aminoorcein.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlpha-aminoorcein.png"}],"sounds":{}}}